---
import { externalLinks } from "@features/shared/config/site";
---
<section id="story" aria-labelledby="story-heading" class="scroll-mt-8 border-t border-hairline">
  <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-narrow flex-col gap-7 px-4 py-24 text-center sm:px-6 sm:py-30">
    <h2 id="story-heading" class="eyebrow font-sans text-muted">A life in deep listening</h2>
    <p class="font-display text-[28px] leading-[1.45] sm:text-[34px]">
      Gianluigi Gasparetti (1958–2013) was the Italian ambient composer best known as Oöphoi. He was also the
      editor of <a href={externalLinks.deepListenings}><em>Deep Listenings</em></a>, an Italian magazine devoted to
      ambient and deep atmospheric music, where he published interviews with many renowned ambient artists,
      including Steve Roach and Michael Stearns.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-5 text-left text-[17px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-soft">
      <p>
        His music is static, organic and minimal, with a solid, monolithic feel, often set in a meditative and
        spiritual context. Built from synths, singing bowls, flutes and processed voices, his recordings unfold
        through slight harmonic variations.
      </p>
      <p>
        A music collector since childhood, Gianluigi began his own experiments in 1995 with basic equipment,
        exploring the shores of deep space ambient. His first album, <cite>Three Lights at the End of the World</cite>,
        composed in 1995, was released in 1996 on Hic Sunt Leones, the label of the Italian ambient artist Alio Die.
      </p>
      <p>
        He played instruments such as synths and samplers, as well as singing bowls, flutes, percussion (wood
        chimes, stones, shells and crystal chimes) and processed voices. All of his music was recorded with
        analog-only equipment in The Kiva, his studio, a former stable in a fifteenth-century stone country house in
        the hills of central Italy.
      </p>
    </div>
    <a href="/biography/" class="self-center text-[15px]">Full biography</a>
  </div>
</section>
